C5 Scope: 8-BALL Deployment Identity and Generated Page Tree

Purpose

This C5 work exists because 8-BALL will fail if the installer cannot reliably identify both:

1. the selected model, and
2. the deployment type/profile that model belongs to.

The repo must stop treating generated catalog output as only flat JSON files. The data pipeline must generate a complete folder/page tree that 8.2 and later installer helpers can target deterministically.

This is a generator task, not a manual folder task. The correct result is hundreds or thousands of generated metadata folders, created from the normalized catalog and deployment recommendation data every time the generator runs.

Non-Negotiable Rules

• Do not create or use 02-models.
• Do not create top-level numbered model folders.
• Do not manually create a few example folders and stop.
• Do not store model weights, Ollama blobs, binaries, or installer payloads in the generated models/ tree.
• Do not remove the existing flat generated JSON/CSV outputs.
• Do not let 8.2 guess deployment type from loose text.
• Do not let 8.2 guess model identity from folder names alone.
• Do not report success unless tests and output counts prove the full tree was generated.

Existing Repo Context

Current generation flow:

• src/eight_ball/generate/outputs.py
• src/eight_ball/generate/deployments.py
• config/hardware_profiles.yaml
• config/deployment_tiers.yaml
• data/generated/*.json
• data/normalized/*.json

Current issue:

• Flat generated files exist.
• Deployment recommendation rows exist or are generated.
• The required page/folder tree is missing.
• The local generated deployment_recommendations.json may be stale or malformed in some checkouts, so C5 must validate generated JSON before page generation is trusted.

Required Output Root

Generate all page folders under:

```text
data/generated/pages/
```

The required tree is:

```text
data/generated/pages/
  families/
    <family-slug>/
      info.json
      README.md

  deployment-types/
    <deployment-type-number>/
      info.json
      README.md

  models/
    <model-slug>/
      model.json
      README.md
      3/
        info.json
        README.md
      4/
        info.json
        README.md
      5/
        info.json
        README.md
      6/
        info.json
        README.md
      7/
        info.json
        README.md
```

The model path must be exactly:

```text
data/generated/pages/models/<model-slug>/<deployment-type-number>/
```

Valid deployment type folder names are:

```text
3
4
5
6
7
```

No other numbered deployment folders should be created unless a later documented C-stage expands the contract.

Deployment Type Contract

C5 must add a canonical deployment type mapping file. Recommended path:

```text
config/deployment_types.yaml
```

It must define deployment type numbers 3 through 7 and map them to the underlying hardware profiles/runtime policies that produce recommendations.

Minimum required fields for each deployment type:

```yaml
deployment_types:
  - id: "3"
    slug: "3"
    display_name: "Deployment Type 3"
    hardware_profile_ids: []
    runtime_policy_ids: []
    installer_target: "3"
    description: ""
    minimum_disk_gb: null
    minimum_ram_gb: null
    recommended_ram_gb: null
    minimum_cpu_threads: null
    gpu_required: false
    minimum_vram_gb: null
    recommended_vram_gb: null
```

If the exact business names for deployment types 3 through 7 are not already documented in the repo, do not invent marketing copy. Use conservative internal display names and preserve the numeric IDs as the source of truth.

The generated model deployment folder must carry both:

• deployment_type_id, one of "3", "4", "5", "6", "7"
• model_id, matching the normalized model row

This is the 8.2 safety rail. The installer should be able to read one machine file and know:

• which model was selected
• which deployment type folder applies
• what hardware limits apply
• what Ollama command to run
• whether the model is local, cloud-only, or unsuitable

